# Pin the GPU (Adreno 643) to its highest OPP, 812 MHz.
#
# The default devfreq governor `simple_ondemand` keeps the GPU at 315 MHz when
# idle and only ramps up under load. Measured on hardware 2026-08-11 from
# trans_stat: 803,320 ms spent at 315 MHz against 25,803 ms at 812 MHz, and the
# intermediate steps (450/550/608 MHz) were never used at all. Pinning removes
# the ramp-up delay at the start of every load.
#
# Why `userspace` rather than `min_freq`:
# android.hardware.power-service.glodroid (device/glodroid/hals/power,
# GloDroidPower.h) scans /sys/devices/platform and /sys/devices/system, treats
# every min_freq file it finds as a "DVFS booster", and restores the initial
# value through DevFreq::Boost() once a boost expires. Any min_freq pin is
# therefore erased within a minute or so — verified. Under the `userspace`
# governor the frequency is owned by set_freq, so the power HAL's writes to
# min_freq have no effect and this pin holds.
#
# Order matters: the userspace/ directory only appears once the governor has
# been set, hence the two writes in sequence.
#
# Triggered on sys.boot_completed rather than `on boot`: pinning the GPU during
# start-up gains nothing, and waiting guarantees the devfreq node exists.
#
# Cost: the GPU also sits at 812 MHz while idle. Measured idle temperature with
# this setting is 36-38 °C against a 90 °C CPU trip point, so thermally it is
# irrelevant, but power draw is higher. To disable at runtime:
#   echo simple_ondemand > /sys/class/devfreq/3d00000.gpu/governor
#
# The CPU is deliberately left alone: a run of n=12 showed no difference in
# median frame time between the performance and schedutil governors (127.5 vs
# 126.0 ms).
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    write /sys/class/devfreq/3d00000.gpu/governor userspace
    write /sys/class/devfreq/3d00000.gpu/userspace/set_freq 812000000
